1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,164
A tree stump in the park.

2
00:00:02,164 --> 00:00:03,206
A wooden door.

3
00:00:03,206 --> 00:00:04,248
A cricket bat.

4
00:00:04,248 --> 00:00:05,483
All made of wood.

5
00:00:05,883 --> 00:00:07,797
Look closely at the stump.

6
00:00:07,797 --> 00:00:10,880
Rings inside rings,
like ripples in a pond.

7
00:00:11,330 --> 00:00:13,715
Aarav counted the rings on a fallen tree.

8
00:00:13,715 --> 00:00:16,015
How does a stem get thicker every year?

9
00:00:16,015 --> 00:00:18,229
And why are the rings light and dark?

10
00:00:18,579 --> 00:00:19,956
Two things answer that.

11
00:00:19,956 --> 00:00:23,280
The cambium, a thin layer of
dividing cells that adds wood.

12
00:00:23,280 --> 00:00:25,916
And the seasons, which
print one ring a year.

13
00:00:26,416 --> 00:00:30,873
By the end, you will know the vascular
cambium, annual rings, heartwood

14
00:00:30,873 --> 00:00:33,247
and sapwood, cork cambium and bark.

15
00:00:33,897 --> 00:00:36,141
First, a plant can grow in two ways.

16
00:00:36,491 --> 00:00:39,727
Growing longer, at the
tips, is primary growth.

17
00:00:39,727 --> 00:00:42,046
Growing thicker is secondary growth.

18
00:00:42,496 --> 00:00:44,750
Girth is the distance around the stem.

19
00:00:44,750 --> 00:00:47,180
Most dicots and gymnosperms grow in girth.

20
00:00:47,180 --> 00:00:49,470
Most monocots, like grasses, do not.

21
00:00:50,120 --> 00:00:52,401
Let's zoom into an old tree trunk.

22
00:00:52,751 --> 00:00:56,029
From outside, a thick trunk,
covered in rough bark.

23
00:00:56,529 --> 00:00:57,513
Cut it across.

24
00:00:57,513 --> 00:01:01,006
Rings of wood fill the middle,
with bark around the outside.

25
00:01:01,506 --> 00:01:03,723
Zoom in where wood meets bark.

26
00:01:03,723 --> 00:01:07,389
A thin band of dividing
cells: the vascular cambium.

27
00:01:07,889 --> 00:01:11,751
Inside, it makes secondary xylem,
new wood that carries water.

28
00:01:11,751 --> 00:01:15,177
Outside, it makes secondary
phloem, which carries food.

29
00:01:15,827 --> 00:01:19,510
Now let's watch the cambium
ring form, step by step.

30
00:01:19,860 --> 00:01:20,760
Step one.

31
00:01:20,659 --> 00:01:25,180
In a young dicot stem, a strip of
cambium sits in each vascular bundle,

32
00:01:25,180 --> 00:01:26,876
between xylem and phloem.

33
00:01:26,876 --> 00:01:28,659
The intrafascicular cambium.

34
00:01:29,409 --> 00:01:30,309
Step two.

35
00:01:30,124 --> 00:01:33,482
Medullary ray cells next to
these strips start dividing too.

36
00:01:33,482 --> 00:01:36,779
They form the interfascicular
cambium, between the bundles.

37
00:01:37,529 --> 00:01:38,429
Step three.

38
00:01:38,373 --> 00:01:42,054
The strips join up into one
complete ring, the cambial ring.

39
00:01:42,804 --> 00:01:43,716
Step four.

40
00:01:43,716 --> 00:01:46,471
The ring cuts off cells on both sides.

41
00:01:46,471 --> 00:01:48,828
More inside, as secondary xylem.

42
00:01:48,828 --> 00:01:51,342
Fewer outside, as secondary phloem.

43
00:01:52,092 --> 00:01:52,992
Step five.

44
00:01:52,911 --> 00:01:56,196
The older phloem is pushed
outward and slowly crushed.

45
00:01:56,196 --> 00:01:58,710
The primary xylem stays near the centre.

46
00:01:59,460 --> 00:02:00,603
Remember this.

47
00:02:00,603 --> 00:02:04,002
The cambium adds xylem
inside and phloem outside.

48
00:02:04,602 --> 00:02:06,301
So where do the rings come from?

49
00:02:06,651 --> 00:02:09,255
In spring, the cambium is very active.

50
00:02:09,255 --> 00:02:11,762
It makes many cells with wide vessels.

51
00:02:11,762 --> 00:02:14,874
This light wood is spring
wood, or early wood.

52
00:02:15,324 --> 00:02:17,610
In winter, the cambium slows down.

53
00:02:17,610 --> 00:02:20,332
It makes fewer cells, with narrow vessels.

54
00:02:20,332 --> 00:02:23,889
This darker, denser wood is
autumn wood, or late wood.

55
00:02:24,339 --> 00:02:28,286
One light band and one dark band
together make one annual ring.

56
00:02:28,286 --> 00:02:32,089
Count the rings, and you can
estimate the age of the tree.

57
00:02:32,739 --> 00:02:36,023
Inside a big trunk, the
wood is not all one colour.

58
00:02:36,373 --> 00:02:37,967
The dark centre is heartwood.

59
00:02:37,967 --> 00:02:41,323
Its dead cells are filled with
tannins, resins, oils and gums.

60
00:02:41,323 --> 00:02:44,933
It no longer carries water, but it
is hard, strong and resists insects

61
00:02:44,933 --> 00:02:45,833
and microbes.

62
00:02:46,197 --> 00:02:48,184
The paler outer wood is sapwood.

63
00:02:48,184 --> 00:02:51,053
It carries water and
minerals from root to leaf.

64
00:02:51,503 --> 00:02:55,947
So the heartwood holds the tree up, and
the sapwood keeps the water flowing.

65
00:02:56,597 --> 00:02:57,790
Now the outside.

66
00:02:57,790 --> 00:03:01,667
As the stem widens, its thin
skin, the epidermis, breaks.

67
00:03:01,667 --> 00:03:03,267
A new cover is needed.

68
00:03:03,617 --> 00:03:06,803
So a cork cambium, or
phellogen, forms in the cortex.

69
00:03:06,803 --> 00:03:10,945
It makes cork, or phellem, outside,
and secondary cortex, or phelloderm,

70
00:03:10,945 --> 00:03:11,845
inside.

71
00:03:11,513 --> 00:03:14,157
Together, these three
layers are the periderm.

72
00:03:14,607 --> 00:03:16,574
Bark is an everyday word.

73
00:03:16,574 --> 00:03:21,513
It means every tissue outside the
vascular cambium: the periderm plus the

74
00:03:21,513 --> 00:03:22,846
secondary phloem.

75
00:03:23,446 --> 00:03:24,346
Your turn.

76
00:03:24,239 --> 00:03:26,546
Aarav peels the bark off a dry twig.

77
00:03:26,546 --> 00:03:29,172
Does the secondary
phloem come off with it?

78
00:03:29,172 --> 00:03:30,339
Pause and decide.

79
00:03:33,539 --> 00:03:34,439
Yes!

80
00:03:34,053 --> 00:03:38,621
The secondary phloem lies outside the
vascular cambium, so it is part of

81
00:03:38,621 --> 00:03:39,521
the bark.

82
00:03:40,098 --> 00:03:41,712
Now, a worked example.

83
00:03:41,712 --> 00:03:42,612
A cut log.

84
00:03:42,599 --> 00:03:45,444
Let's name three zones,
from the outside in.

85
00:03:45,794 --> 00:03:51,133
Zone one, the rough outer layer,
everything outside the vascular cambium.

86
00:03:51,133 --> 00:03:52,033
Bark.

87
00:03:52,212 --> 00:03:54,648
Zone two, the pale wood just inside.

88
00:03:54,648 --> 00:03:56,203
It still carries water.

89
00:03:56,203 --> 00:03:57,103
Sapwood.

90
00:03:57,326 --> 00:04:00,656
Zone three, the dark centre,
packed with tannins and resins.

91
00:04:00,656 --> 00:04:01,556
Only support.

92
00:04:01,494 --> 00:04:02,394
Heartwood.

93
00:04:02,610 --> 00:04:05,085
Twelve light and dark pairs of rings?

94
00:04:05,085 --> 00:04:07,461
The tree is about twelve years old.

95
00:04:08,111 --> 00:04:09,573
Now, the exam trap.

96
00:04:09,923 --> 00:04:13,319
Many students think bark is only
the dead cork on the outside.

97
00:04:13,719 --> 00:04:17,027
But bark is every tissue
outside the vascular cambium.

98
00:04:17,027 --> 00:04:20,088
It includes the periderm
and the secondary phloem.

99
00:04:20,738 --> 00:04:22,069
Where is this asked?

100
00:04:22,069 --> 00:04:26,100
As statement questions on spring and
autumn wood, heartwood and sapwood,

101
00:04:26,100 --> 00:04:27,566
and what bark contains.

102
00:04:28,166 --> 00:04:29,327
Exam level now.

103
00:04:29,327 --> 00:04:31,038
Read the four statements.

104
00:04:31,038 --> 00:04:32,652
Which ones are correct?

105
00:04:32,652 --> 00:04:33,862
Pause and solve.

106
00:04:39,262 --> 00:04:40,447
The answer is A.

107
00:04:40,447 --> 00:04:42,469
Statements one, three and four.

108
00:04:42,969 --> 00:04:44,040
Two is wrong.

109
00:04:44,040 --> 00:04:46,222
Heartwood does not conduct water.

110
00:04:46,222 --> 00:04:47,234
Sapwood does.

111
00:04:47,734 --> 00:04:49,492
One, three and four are right.

112
00:04:49,492 --> 00:04:53,120
Spring wood has wide vessels, phellogen
makes cork outside, and medullary

113
00:04:53,120 --> 00:04:55,316
ray cells form the
interfascicular cambium.

114
00:04:55,916 --> 00:04:57,457
Here is your recap.

115
00:04:57,457 --> 00:04:59,041
Screenshot this card.

116
00:05:02,240 --> 00:05:04,426
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

117
00:05:04,826 --> 00:05:05,726
One.

118
00:05:05,298 --> 00:05:08,436
On which side does the
cambium make secondary xylem?

119
00:05:09,336 --> 00:05:10,236
Two.

120
00:05:09,710 --> 00:05:11,106
What makes one annual ring?

121
00:05:12,006 --> 00:05:12,906
Three.

122
00:05:12,498 --> 00:05:14,572
Name the three layers of the periderm.

123
00:05:15,672 --> 00:05:20,116
Next time: the skin on your hand and
the inside of your cheek are both

124
00:05:20,116 --> 00:05:21,259
covering layers.

125
00:05:21,259 --> 00:05:22,665
How are they built?

126
00:05:22,665 --> 00:05:24,872
We find out in epithelial tissue.
